{0 Zulip bindings}

These libraries provide Zulip Server 12.2 bindings using Fetch/httpz for HTTP,
Eio for concurrency, Jsont for JSON, and Xdge for named profiles.

The
{{:https://tangled.org/anil.recoil.org/ocaml-zulip/blob/main/example/README.md}
example tutorial} builds a bot from message handling through commands, rooms,
plugins, and persistent state. Standalone examples cover one-shot requests and
an injected Fetch mock.

{1 Libraries}

{!Zulip} contains protocol types and codecs. {!Zulip.Id} provides nominally
distinct identifiers for users, channels, messages, groups, folders,
attachments, and scheduled work. Extensible flags and event operations retain
future wire values. {!Zulip.Message.type-destination} preserves a channel topic or
the participants in a direct message.

{!Zulip_eio} provides {!Zulip_eio.Transport}, {!Zulip_eio.Client},
{!Zulip_eio.Profile}, endpoint modules, and {!Zulip_eio.Event_queue}.
Operations live in resource modules such as {!Zulip_eio.Messages},
{!Zulip_eio.Attachments}, {!Zulip_eio.Channels}, and
{!Zulip_eio.Scheduled_messages}. Results preserve structured
{!Zulip_eio.Error.t} failures. {!Zulip_eio.Error.or_raise} converts them to
exceptions when required.

{!Zulip_bot} provides bot specifications, commands, events, rooms, send
handles, and plugin state. {!Zulip_bot_cli.Main} supplies Cmdliner entry points.

{1 Transport and event queues}

Construct one transport from the application's Eio environment and reuse it.
Ordinary requests default to a 30-second deadline when a clock is available.
Polling uses the server's advertised timeout with an additional margin.
Decoded JSON responses have body and nesting limits. Downloads stream into a
caller-owned sink and may follow HTTPS redirects while keeping credentials
scoped to the Zulip origin.

GET and HEAD requests may be retried by the transport. Mutations are not
automatically replayed after connection failures or HTTP 5xx responses. The
bot sender may retry an explicit rate-limit rejection.

{!Zulip_eio.Event_queue.get_events} returns a batch without advancing its
queue's acknowledgement cursor. Accept events in order, then call
{!Zulip_eio.Event_queue.ack} with the cumulative prefix accepted from that
batch. Repeated acknowledgement never moves the cursor backwards. A queue
permits one outstanding poll, and event identifiers apply only to their queue
generation.

The bot collector admits each event to its bounded dispatcher before
acknowledging that event's prefix. Handlers run in order within a channel or
direct-message room. Independent rooms may proceed concurrently. A blocked
handler eventually backpressures collection.

{1 Bot lifecycle and sends}

Build an immutable {!Zulip_bot.Bot.type-spec} by applying plugins of type
[spec -> spec]. Registration order determines handler order. Exceptions reach
the error hook and do not imply replay. Eio cancellation propagates.

{!Zulip_bot.Context.v} accepts an existing client and identity.
{!Zulip_bot.Context.connect} resolves a profile, looks up the identity, and
opens the profile's plugin store. The caller-supplied context switch owns the
sender. {!Zulip_bot.Bot.run} scopes the collector and room workers.
{!Zulip_bot.Bot.stop} requests collector termination and cancels queued room
work, but leaves the context sender alive.

Releasing the context switch wakes admissions blocked by a full sender queue.
It settles queued handles as {!Zulip_bot.Sent.outcome.constructor-Cancelled}
and in-flight handles as
{!Zulip_bot.Sent.outcome.constructor-Indeterminate} with no error. Handles that
already reached a terminal outcome retain it.

{!Zulip_bot.Sent.await} returns [`Done outcome] when the handle reaches a
terminal {!Zulip_bot.Sent.outcome}. It returns [`Timed_out] when only the
observation deadline expires, leaving the send running. A lost response may
follow a successful server write and yields an indeterminate outcome.

Default activation accepts one-to-one direct messages and explicit mentions.
It ignores messages from the bot itself and known bots. Additional event types
can be observed through custom handlers. {!Zulip.Event_payload} provides typed
views of supported events while preserving unknown families and future fields.

Rooms use channel or direct-recipient identifiers as keys. Reply destinations
retain the current topic or complete participant set. Edits and reactions use
a bounded message-location cache with API lookup on misses. Malformed supported
events reach the malformed-event hook.

{1 Message retrieval}

{!Zulip_eio.Messages.get_messages} separates history ranges from selected-ID
queries. A range requires nonnegative counts before and after its anchor.
Omitting the anchor selects {!Zulip_eio.Messages.anchor.constructor-Newest}.
{!Zulip_eio.Messages.anchor.constructor-Date} selects the first message at or
after its ISO 8601 date or datetime. A selected-ID query omits every range
argument. Zulip returns only requested messages that exist, are accessible,
and match the optional narrow.

{1 Persistence and webhooks}

{!Zulip_bot.Plugin_store} provides typed, namespaced values using Jsont codecs.
Updates serialize within one process, and file replacement is atomic. Delivery
state is not persistent. Restarting registers a fresh queue and can miss events
received while the bot was offline.

{!Zulip_bot.Webhook.handle} validates a bounded outgoing-webhook envelope and
admits it to the bot dispatcher. Its HTTP acknowledgement confirms dispatch
acceptance. It does not confirm handler completion or reply delivery.

{1 Deployment and verification}

The
{{:https://tangled.org/anil.recoil.org/ocaml-zulip/blob/main/HOSTED_BOTS.md}
hosted bot guide} covers profiles, preflight checks, Zulip Cloud, and service
supervision. The
{{:https://tangled.org/anil.recoil.org/ocaml-zulip/blob/main/API_COVERAGE.md}
coverage inventory} maps Python SDK helpers and additional Zulip 12.2
operations to the OCaml API. The
{{:https://tangled.org/anil.recoil.org/ocaml-zulip/blob/main/test/integration/README.md}
Docker guide} describes the isolated real-server test workflow.
